course: dictionary
lesson: 664
title: Bait: fish, traps and tricks
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Bait» is food put on a hook or in a trap to catch something. The same word can also mean anything used to tempt someone into a trick. «Mice will eat cheese, but they prefer grains, seeds and sweet foods, so people baiting traps usually use peanut butter.» Here bait is the food inside the trap.
explain: «His dad said that links like that often carry viruses or steal your account, and the "infinite coins" are the bait on the hook.» The free coins look tempting, but they are there to catch you. When someone takes the bait, they fall for the trick.
explain: Context clues help you pick the right meaning. Look at the words near bait: trap, hook, link, free coins. Then try your meaning in the sentence. If «the free coins are the trick that tempts you» still makes sense, you have found it.
fact: Bait is food put on a hook or in a trap to catch fish or animals.
fact: Bait can also mean something used to tempt a person into a trap or trick.
fact: To bait means to put bait on a hook or in a trap, as in «people baiting traps usually use peanut butter».
fact: To bait can also mean to tease or annoy someone on purpose to make them angry.
fact: Clickbait is a post or link that tricks you into clicking, and rage bait is a post made to anger you on purpose.
exercise: «Mice will eat cheese, but they prefer grains, seeds and sweet foods, so people baiting traps usually use peanut butter.» What is the bait in this sentence? | The mouse | The cheese | *Peanut butter | The trap
exercise: «The "infinite coins" are the bait on the hook.» What does bait mean here? | *A trick that tempts you to click | A tasty snack | A type of hook | A worm for fishing
exercise: Which word means to tease someone on purpose to make them angry? | *To bait | To bake | To brake | To bathe
test: What does baiting mean in «people baiting traps»? | Cleaning a trap | Catching fish with a net | *Putting bait in traps | Buying cheese
test: What does «take the bait» mean? | Go fishing | *Fall for a trick | Eat a snack | Win a prize
exam: Which is the best way to check the meaning of an unknown word? | Guess the longest answer | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Ignore the whole sentence | Ask someone else to do the work
